partly over the field of ''with''.] A preposition of extremely various use, primarily meaning to, without implication, in itself, of motion. {{smaller|It expresses position attained by motion to, and hence contact, contiguity, or coincidence, actual or approximate, in space or time. Being less restricted as to relative position than other prepositions, it may in different constructions assume their office, and so become equivalent, according to the context, to ''in'', ''on'', ''near'', ''by'', ''about'', ''under'', ''over'', ''through'', ''from'', ''to'', ''toward'', etc.}}

'''1.''' Of simple local position: (''a'') With verbs of rest (''be'', ''live'', etc.): In, on, near, by, etc., according to the context: denoting usually a place conceived of as a mere point: as, ''at'' the center, ''at'' the top, ''at'' the corner, ''at'' the end, ''at'' the next station, ''at'' the bend of the river, ''at'' the north pole, ''at'' No. 48 Main street, etc. {{smaller|So with names of towns, etc.: as, ''at'' Stratford, ''at'' Lexington, etc.; but if the city is of great size ''in'' is commonly used: as, ''in'' London, ''in'' Paris, ''in'' New York; unless, again, the city is conceived of as a mere geographical point: as, our financial interests center ''at'' New York. The place implied by ''at'' may be left indeterminate, with a reference rather to condition than to mere location: as, ''at'' school, ''at'' college, ''at'' court, ''at'' sea, etc. ''At'' may also express personal proximity: as, ''at'' one’s side, ''at'' one’s heels, ''at'' one’s elbow, etc. ''At hand'', near by, has lost its personal reference.}}
{{smaller block|I don’t believe there’s a circulating library in Bath I ha’n’t been ''at''.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|Muley Abul Hassan, ''at'' the head of a powerful force, had hurried from Granada.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 20.}}}}
{{smaller block|He [Don Juan de Vera] was armed ''at'' all points, gallantly mounted, and followed by a moderate but well-appointed retinue.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 10.}}}}
(''b'') With verbs of motion: (1) Through, by (implying a starting-point or a point where a thing enters or departs): as, to enter ''at'' the window, to go out ''at'' the back door. (2) From (imply-

readings.c1s3:
ing a source from which a thing comes or where it is sought): as, to receive ill treatment ''at'' their hands. (3) To, toward (implying a stopping-point, a position attained or aimed at): as, to come ''at'', to get ''at'', to aim ''at'', fire ''at'', shoot ''at'', drive ''at'', point ''at'', look ''at'', shout ''at'', reach ''at'', snatch ''at'', clutch ''at'', etc.; also be ''at'' when it implies effort directed toward a thing.
{{smaller block|No doubt but they will soone answer that all these things they seeke ''at'' God’s hands.
{{right|''Milton'', Def. of Humb. Remonstrants.}}
There is no way of coming ''at'' a true theory of society but by inquiring into the nature of its component individuals.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 28.}}
What you can drive ''at'', unless you mean to quarrel with me, I cannot conceive!
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iv. 3.}}
In spite of his former submissions and promises, Latimer was ''at'' it again.
{{right|''R. W. Dixon'', Hist. Church of Eng., iii.}}
Who but Henry could have been aware of what his father was ''at?''
{{right|''Jane Austen'', Northanger Abbey, p. 172.}}}}
'''2.''' Of circumstantial position, state, condition, manner, environment, etc., in a great variety of relations developed from the local sense: as, ''at'' dinner, ''at'' play, ''at'' work, ''at'' service, ''at'' right angles, ''at'' full length, ''at'' odds, ''at'' ease, ''at'' war, ''at'' peace, ''at'' will, ''at'' pleasure, ''at'' discretion, etc.
{{smaller block|They let her goe ''at'' will, and wander waies unknowne.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. viii. 49.}}
<poem>I have brought you a new song will make you laugh,
Though you were ''at'' your prayers.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', False One, i. 1.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Really, sir, you have the advantage of me:—I don’t remember ever to have had the honour—my name is Saunderson, ''at'' your service.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, v. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Success would place a rich town ''at'' their mercy.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 35.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ship in which he [Goldsmith] had taken his passage, having got a fair wind while he was ''at'' a party of pleasure, had sailed without him.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Goldsmith.}}}}

'''3.''' Of relative position: implying a point in an actual or possible series, and hence used of degree, price, time, order, occasion, etc.: as, ''at'' the beginning, ''at'' the third house from the corner, ''at'' nine years of age, ''at'' seventy degrees in the shade, ''at'' four dollars a yard, ''at'' ten cents a pound, ''at'' half past six, ''at'' midnight, ''at'' first, ''at'' last, etc.

{{smaller block|I’ll take them ''at'' your own price.
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|''At'' present, if you please, we’ll drop the subject.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Duenna, i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|In passing through the gate of Elvira, however, he accidentally broke his lance against the arch. ''At'' this, certain of his nobles turned pale, for they regarded it as an evil omen.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 108.}}}}

{{smaller block|[In all uses, especially in those last mentioned, ''at'' is very frequent in idiomatical phrases: as, ''at all'', ''at most'', ''at least'', ''at last'', ''at length'', ''at any rate'', ''at stake'', ''at one'', ''at once'', ''at large'', ''at present'', etc., for which see the principal words, ''all'', ''most'', ''least'', etc.]}}

'''4†.''' With the infinitive: To.

{{smaller block|<poem>Faire gan him pray
''At'' ride thurgh Ingland.</poem>
{{right|''Minot'', Poems (ed. Ritson), p. 40.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Now only dialectal, but common in Middle English, and the regular use in Scandinavian, to which the English use is due. A relic of this use remains in ''ado'', originally ''at''}}

